course: dictionary
lesson: 6370
title: Mud: more than dirt
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Mud is wet, soft, sticky earth. Read the words around it to find its meaning: «Outside, the fresh tracks in the mud lead straight to the Wild Wood.» Tracks are marks left by feet or wheels, and they are pressed into soft ground, so mud must be that wet earth.
explain: Sometimes mud means something different. «Throw mud» is informal, and it means saying unkind things to make someone look bad. The clue is in the word throw: you cannot really throw a feeling or an insult, so the phrase must be figurative.
explain: Other phrases use mud too, and they do not mean what the words say. «As clear as mud» means not clear at all, and «your name is mud» means you are in trouble. When a phrase seems silly, swap in a meaning and check whether the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Mud is soil mixed with water.
fact: When mud dries, it can harden, which is how mud bricks are made.
fact: Pigs and hippos roll in mud to keep cool and protect their skin.
fact: «Covered in mud» means dirty all over with wet earth.
fact: «Stuck in the mud» means unable to move forward or make progress.
exercise: «Outside, the fresh tracks in the mud lead straight to the Wild Wood.» What is mud here? | *Wet, soft, sticky earth | A type of cake | Cold water in a puddle | Dry dust on the path
exercise: Sam says, «You threw mud at Priya in the group chat.» What does throw mud mean? | Make a muddy pie | Clean the playground | Kick a ball into a puddle | *Say unkind things to make someone look bad
exercise: «Then he dived full length in the mud and saved a penalty.» What does full length suggest? | *Stretched all the way out, with his body flat | Crawling slowly | Jumping very high | Standing still
test: Why do pigs roll in mud? | To make mud bricks | To hide from the Wild Wood | To clean their ears | *To keep cool and protect their skin
test: «Your name is mud» means what? | You like playing in puddles | Your name is written in soil | Your name is famous everywhere | *You are in trouble
exam: «As clear as mud» means what? | Dried hard like a brick | *Not clear at all | Very clear and bright | Covered in dirt
